# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for scripts/sanitize_upgrade.sh.
#
# Only reports traced to third-party code (Minuit2, GSL, the OpenMP runtime) may be suppressed, never a report
# whose racing accesses are in AZURE2's own sources: those are findings (docs/upgrade/sanitizer_findings.md) and
# are fixed on a fix/ branch.  Each entry carries, above it, the report it silences and why that report is not an
# AZURE2 race.  Format: https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerSuppressions

# LLVM libomp, worker suspend/resume.  A worker initializes its own suspend mutex (pthread_mutex_init in
# __kmp_suspend_initialize_thread) and the thread that wakes it locks that mutex (__kmp_resume_template); the
# order between the two is set by libomp's internal atomic flags, which TSan does not see because libomp is
# not instrumented (libarcher annotates OpenMP constructs, not the runtime's own bookkeeping).  Both stacks are
# entirely inside libomp; AZURE2 appears only as the frame that opened the parallel region.  Seen on every run
# (T1 calculation, clang 23.1.4, libomp 23.1.4, 8 threads).
race:__kmp_suspend_initialize_thread
race:__kmp_resume_template

# The same report where libomp carries no symbols (GitHub's ubuntu-24.04 runner, clang 18, libomp.so.5: its
# frames print "<null>", so the two names above match nothing).  Seen in every step of the CI's thread job,
# 2026-11-15: pthread_mutex_lock called from libomp on the main thread against pthread_mutex_init called from
# libomp on a worker, the heap block allocated by libomp; AZURE2 appears only as the frame that opened the
# parallel region (ChannelRowFill.cpp).  Two entries, neither tried on a machine of the upgrade (no clang there):
#
# 1. called_from_lib: TSan ignores the pthread calls that libomp itself makes (its interceptors, for the time of
#    the call).  Memory accesses of AZURE2 inside a parallel region stay instrumented, and so do AZURE2's own
#    locks (std::mutex locks from AZURE2's code, not from libomp); the order of OpenMP constructs comes from
#    libarcher's annotations, which do not pass through the interceptors.  It names the module, so it holds
#    whatever the runtime's bookkeeping does next.  A module entry of the other kind (race:libomp.so) would hide
#    every race inside a parallel region, whose stacks all pass through libomp, and is not used.
# 2. The interceptor of the initialization itself.  AZURE2 never calls pthread_mutex_init (its locks are
#    std::mutex, which has no run-time initialization), so a report with this frame cannot be a race between
#    accesses of AZURE2.  Not anchored: the first form of this entry, race:^pthread_mutex_init$, matched nothing
#    on the CI (the seventh push: the same report in every step), most likely because the name is matched as the
#    symbol table has it, with the interceptor's prefix, and the report prints it without.
called_from_lib:libomp.so
race:pthread_mutex_init

# libarcher's own lock around an "omp critical" (clang 18's libarcher: for each critical section it keeps a
# std::mutex of its own in a map, locks it when the section is entered and unlocks it when the section is left).
# Seen on the CI, 2026-11-15, in the first run in which the report above was silent (the ninth push; six steps, all
# of T1 and two of T3): "double lock of a mutex", pthread_mutex_lock called from libarcher on a worker, the mutex in
# a 56-byte heap block allocated by libarcher and first locked by libarcher on the main thread, AZURE2 only as the
# place of the critical section (ec_progress, ExternalCaptureSetup.cpp).  The mutex is the tool's, not AZURE2's;
# why TSan counts it as still held is NOT understood (it may follow from called_from_lib above, or have stood
# behind the first report all along: not separated).  The entry silences mutex-misuse reports (double lock, bad
# unlock) with a frame in libarcher and nothing else: a misuse of a std::mutex of AZURE2 has no such frame, and
# data races are of another kind (race:).  Not tried on a machine of the upgrade.
mutex:libarcher.so
